<SiteLayout title="The Beginner’s Bible — Kraig Spear" description="Historical iOS work at HarperCollins: an interactive storybook and the StoryBook Builder authoring tool, 2012–2015.">
  <section class="hero wrap" aria-labelledby="bible-title">
    <a class="text-link" href="/#work">← Selected work</a>
    <p class="eyebrow">Historical project · 2012–2015</p>
    <h1 id="bible-title" class="case-title">The Beginner’s Bible</h1>
    <div class="hero-bottom"><p>Stories to read, touch, and play. A shipped iOS app that brought illustrated stories, narration, games, coloring, and puzzles together for young readers.</p></div>
    <dl class="project-facts">
      <div><dt>My role</dt><dd>Senior Developer of Mobile Technologies</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Publisher</dt><dd>HarperCollins Publishers</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Technology at the time</dt><dd>Objective-C · UIKit · AVFoundation · CoreGraphics</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>
  <figure class="case-image wrap">
    <img src="/images/beginners-bible.jpg" width="1920" height="1440" alt="A reader holds an iPad showing The Beginner’s Bible illustrated story selection, with a child nearby." fetchpriority="high" />
    <figcaption>The shipped storybook experience, shown in its original project imagery.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <section class="about wrap" aria-labelledby="experience-title">
    <p class="eyebrow">The experience</p>
    <div>
      <h2 id="experience-title">More than turning pages.</h2>
      <p>The app paired illustrated stories and narration with interactions children could explore: games, coloring, and puzzles. The content grew to more than 400 pages, making the work both an iOS application and a substantial interactive-content project.</p>
      <p>I developed the iOS app and its technical architecture, working with design and editorial colleagues to bring the experience to the App Store. The challenge extended beyond implementing individual scenes: the team also needed a practical way to assemble and maintain them.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="about wrap" aria-labelledby="builder-title">
    <p class="eyebrow">The engineering</p>
    <div>
      <h2 id="builder-title">Building the tools to build the book</h2>
      <p>I created StoryBook Builder, a visual authoring tool for placing artwork and configuring interactions and narration. Instead of treating each story page as a separate layout task in app code, the tool gave the team a visual workspace for assembling the content.</p>
      <p>The original image below shows the authoring workflow: a selected object on the story canvas, with its interaction type and sound configured alongside it. The corresponding illustrated page is visible on the iPad.</p>
      <p>That investment connected two parts of the work: the native app that delivered the experience, and the production tool that made a large collection of interactive pages practical to build.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
